# Security First

## Objective

Treat security as a default requirement, not an opt-in — especially for input handling, authentication, and data exposure.

## Core Principles

- Never trust client-supplied input; validate and sanitize at the boundary.
- Use parameterized queries — never string-concatenate SQL.
- Never log or expose secrets, tokens, passwords, or PII in error messages/logs.
- Apply the principle of least privilege to new permissions, roles, or API scopes.
- Default to HTTPS/TLS and secure cookie flags where applicable.

## Requirements

- Validate and sanitize all external input (forms, query params, headers, file uploads).
- Use existing auth/authorization middleware instead of writing ad-hoc checks.
- Escape output appropriately for its context (HTML, SQL, shell) to prevent injection.
- Any new endpoint gets an explicit authorization check, not just authentication.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Concatenating user input directly into a SQL query or shell command.
- Returning raw stack traces or internal error details to end users.
- Storing passwords or tokens in plaintext or reversible encoding.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ All new external input is validated/sanitized.
✓ No secrets, tokens, or PII appear in logs, errors, or committed code.
✓ New endpoints/actions have explicit authorization checks.

## Golden Rule

When in doubt, choose the more restrictive, more explicit security default.
